    <!--
      The rationale shown before Android's own POST_NOTIFICATIONS dialog, on API 33+ only. It says
      what the permission buys (the media notification, which is the only way back into a
      backgrounded video) and when declining is the right answer — because the ask now arrives on
      the library screen, before the user has played anything to give it context.
    -->
    <string name="notification_permission_title">Show playback notifications?</string>
    <string name="notification_permission_body">While a video is playing, Jellyshelf can post a notification you can pause and skip from — and tap to jump straight back into the video after leaving the app.\n\nIt is the only thing this permission is used for: no alerts, nothing when the app is idle. Decline and playback still works, but a video you leave has no controls and no way back to it.</string>
    <!-- Paired with local_network_permission_allow the way the two "Not now" strings below are
         paired: one word, two unrelated grants, and a translator must be free to move either. -->
    <string name="notification_permission_allow" tools:ignore="DuplicateStrings">Allow</string>
    <!--
      Deliberately its own string rather than a reuse of update_dismiss, which happens to read the
      same today: these are two unrelated refusals, and a translator (or a copy change) must be
      free to move one without silently moving the other.
    -->
    <string name="notification_permission_dismiss" tools:ignore="DuplicateStrings">Not now</string>

    <!--
      The rationale shown before Android's own ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK dialog, on API 36+ only.

      This permission is unusual in that its absence produces no error: Android drops the
      connection rather than refusing it, so the app waits out its own request timeout and reports
      the server as unreachable. The body therefore has to describe a symptom the user may already
      have seen and blamed on their server, which is why it is more concrete than the notification
      one — and has to say what is *not* being done, since "let this app see devices on your
      network" is a fair thing to hesitate over.
    -->
    <string name="local_network_permission_title">Allow Jellyshelf to reach your server?</string>
    <string name="local_network_permission_body">A Jellyfin server on your own network — anything at a 192.168, 10.x or .local address — is behind a separate Android permission from Android 16 onwards, on top of ordinary internet access.

Jellyshelf uses it for one thing: connecting to the server address you enter. It does not scan your network or look for anything else on it.

Without it, Android does not refuse the connection, it silently drops it — so signing in sits there and then reports the server as unreachable, even though the server is fine.</string>
    <string name="local_network_permission_allow" tools:ignore="DuplicateStrings">Allow</string>
    <!-- Its own string for the same reason notification_permission_dismiss is: unrelated refusals. -->
    <string name="local_network_permission_dismiss" tools:ignore="DuplicateStrings">Not now</string>
</resources>
